# Test Conventions

## File Naming

- Test files: `src/test/<moduleName>.test.ts` — one test file per source module
- Test runner: `src/test/runTests.ts` — Mocha entry point; do not modify casually
- Mock setup: `src/test/setup.ts` — global vscode module mocks, loaded before all tests

## Mock Setup (setup.ts)

`setup.ts` uses mock-require to replace the `vscode` module with a comprehensive mock object before any test file runs.

Mocks provided:

- `window` — showInformationMessage, showErrorMessage, showWarningMessage, showQuickPick, createWebviewPanel, createOutputChannel, createStatusBarItem
- `commands` — registerCommand, executeCommand
- `languages` — registerCodeLensProvider, registerDefinitionProvider, registerDiagnosticProvider, createDiagnosticCollection
- `workspace` — getConfiguration, findFiles, openTextDocument, onDidSaveTextDocument, onDidChangeConfiguration, createFileSystemWatcher
- `Uri`, `Range`, `Position`, `Diagnostic`, `DiagnosticSeverity`, `TreeItem`, `EventEmitter`, `ConfigurationTarget`

Rules:

- NEVER import the real `vscode` module in test files — always rely on the mocks provided by setup.ts
- If a test needs a mock behavior that setup.ts does not provide, EXTEND setup.ts — do not create local per-file mocks
- Local mock overrides create silent divergence and cause test failures that are hard to trace

## Pure Function Extraction

Every parser, transformer, or output formatter MUST be a pure function that accepts string input and returns structured data.

Example:

```typescript
parseFrontmatter(text: string): { kind: string; name: string; nameLine: number } | null
```

- Pure functions are testable without any vscode dependency — test them directly without mocks
- If you find yourself needing complex mocks to test a function, extract the logic into a pure function first
- Putting parsing logic inside a class method that also touches vscode APIs couples two concerns — split them

## Test Structure

```typescript
describe('ClassName or functionName', () => {
  it('should handle normal case', () => { ... });
  it('should handle edge case', () => { ... });
  it('should return null for invalid input', () => { ... }); // negative test
});
```

- Use `describe` for grouping by module or function name
- Use `it` with a plain English description of the scenario
- Each `it` block tests exactly one behavior

## Mandatory Requirements

- Every new provider or command MUST have a matching test file in `src/test/`
- Every parser function MUST have at least one positive test case AND at least one negative test case
- Tests MUST be deterministic — no network calls, no real filesystem access, no timers
- Test names must be descriptive enough to identify the failure without reading the test body
